.0.0:8181.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8ac9311d39014d66\",\"cmd-ff0c096a8c7d44fc\",\"cmd-feffa872841c4bf2\",\"cmd-9505eada721c4391\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"apparmor-enforcing\",\"requirement\":\"Mandatory access control (AppArmor on this Ubuntu image) remains in enforcing mode while the app is served\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"aa-status showed AppArmor active with 24 profiles enforcing prior to deployment, the playbook explicitly verified enforcing mode both before and after the deploy tasks (both reported ok), and enforcing mode with the same 24 enforce-mode profile count was re-confirmed immediately after node1's restart while the service was live.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b25f4d7753ab4931\",\"cmd-8ac9311d39014d66\",\"cmd-ff0c096a8c7d44fc\",\"cmd-9505eada721c4391\"]},{\"id\":\"ansible-implementation\",\"requirement\":\"The solution is implemented using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A 129-line Ansible playbook (site.yml) was authored and executed via ansible-playbook, producing a full task recap (ok=12 changed=5 failed=0) covering AppArmor, app deployment, and the systemd unit. Ansible was installed and run locally on node1 rather than remotely from node2, because node1 had no sshd; the task instruction does not mandate remote execution from a specific control node, so this is an acceptable implementation choice.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6743999d147849fc\",\"cmd-62fdb9851a684616\",\"cmd-8ac9311d39014d66\"]},{\"id\":\"node1-restart-evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart-evidence protocol on node1: pre-restart healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered service/data state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Boot_id f9bf293d captured pre-restart alongside a healthy baseline (AppArmor enforcing, service active, curl success). systemctl reboot was acknowledged, the node went unreachable (connection reset), then came back with a new boot_id 7e7304b2, matching the fixed after_executor snapshot. Post-restart checks confirmed AppArmor still enforcing, inventory-app active/enabled, and a successful curl.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-52cfeb9b0a3d4d27\",\"cmd-bb80554732ab4319\",\"cmd-50845b05a2774827\",\"cmd-5a91fa5e5e1c4c5c\",\"cmd-9505eada721c4391\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"node2-restart-evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart-evidence protocol on node2: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Boot_id 489b0bb4 captured pre-restart with Ansible confirmed present. systemctl reboot acknowledged, node timed out mid-reboot, then returned with new boot_id 41677490, matching the fixed after_executor snapshot. Post-restart, Ansible was functional and a cross-node curl to node1's inventory endpoint succeeded, confirming recovered cluster connectivity.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ae81fbd6db9a4268\",\"cmd-6216038b21ed46a0\",\"cmd-9c7eb0e2db514466\",\"cmd-e91a812b412e45d9\",\"cmd-0f34b2b83591458a\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing executor commands map clearly to the task or its mandated restart protocol: installing Ansible on node1 (necessary prerequisite once no sshd was found for remote execution), writing/running the playbook (delivers the port-8181 service and AppArmor checks), and the two node reboots (mandated restart-evidence protocol). No unnecessary mutations, abandoned files, failed services, or unsafe exposures are evident; after_executor shows no failed systemd units on either node and dpkg package health is clean. The only residue is expected (new inventory-app files/service, ephemeral systemd-private tmp dirs tied to the new boot).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6743999d147849fc\",\"cmd-62fdb9851a684616\",\"cmd-8ac9311d39014d66\",\"cmd-bb80554732ab4319\",\"cmd-6216038b21ed46a0\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes are satisfied: node1 serves the inventory app on TCP 8181 via a systemd-managed Python server deployed by Ansible, AppArmor remained in enforcing mode throughout deployment and after a full node1 reboot, and the provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was cleanly demonstrated on both nodes with matching boot-identity transitions and recovered health/connectivity. The only notable deviation is that Ansible ran locally on node1 (rather than remotely from node2 as control node) because node1 lacked an SSH server; this is a reasonable adaptation and not a contract violation since the task only required an Ansible-based implementation.\",\"limitations\":[\"Ansible executed locally on node1 rather than remotely from node2 (the nominal control_node) because node1 had no sshd; the authored topology implies node2-to-node1 orchestration, but the task text itself does not require this specific execution path.\",\"AppArmor enforcement was checked via profile counts (aa-status) rather than confirming the inventory-app process itself runs under a dedicated confined profile; enforcing mode at the system level is verified, but per-application AppArmor confinement was not separately demonstrated.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"port-8181","requirement":"Inventory application is served over TCP port 8181 on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Playbook deployed a systemd-managed Python HTTP server bound to 0.0.0.0:8181. curl on node1 and from node2 both returned the JSON inventory payload before and after node1's restart, and the after_executor listening-sockets snapshot directly shows python3 listening on 0.0.0.0:8181.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8ac9311d39014d66","cmd-ff0c096a8c7d44fc","